BOPAlgo_WireSplitter: a vertex the edges of a face meet at points a period
apart, with no closed edge there, is told apart by those points
(mitcad#2170, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#463).

The case: a rod with three thin fins, read from a STEP file. The rod's
side (a cylinder) is one face without a seam: its boundary runs down one
line of the cylinder (u = 0), round the rod, and back up beside the fins
(u just short of 2 pi), and it reaches u = 2 pi only at one vertex, where
two fins meet the rod; there it passes that vertex a second time, on the
line it ran down (u = 0). An inner wire of two edges, where the third
fin's knife edge grazes the rod, touches the boundary at that vertex
(u = 0). Six edges meet at the vertex, at two points of the face's
parameters a period apart, and none of them is closed on the face. The
face is valid. A union of the finned rod with a ring or a triangle that
trims the face splits it with the boolean's face builder (the vertex is
used more than twice, so BuildDraftFace does not keep its wires).

The wire splitter (Path) treats a vertex as one point unless an edge
there is closed on the face or degenerated (aVertMap). Arriving at this
vertex at u = 2 pi, it closed a loop at the earlier visit at u = 0, and it
chose the edge to go on along from the edges leaving both points, by
angles measured at each edge's own point. Two of the five loops came out
closed in 3d but a period apart in 2d: their faces were not closed in the
face's parameters and could not be oriented (BRepCheck_NotClosed,
BRepCheck_UnorientableShape), and the union of the 26 bodies of the
reported design was invalid.

In SplitBlock, on a surface periodic or closed in u or v (the face's
adaptor), a vertex used by more than two edges, none of them closed on the
face or degenerated, whose edges meet it at points more than half a period
apart, is marked (aVertCopies), where the face's own wires (the face given
to the face builder, valid, its curves meeting each other) also meet it
at points a period apart. A vertex the boundary passes once, where an edge
whose curve the boolean made (a section, a split shared with another
face's edge, on a face spanning more than the period) lies a period from
the boundary, is not marked: the vertex the join of a pad flush with a
body's merged cylindrical walls met so came out without the pad when it
was (geometry.suite.boolean, mitcad#964's case). The face's own vertex is
matched with the vertex in the split where it is that vertex or lies
within their tolerances of it: where another argument's vertex coincides
with it, the pave filler puts a new vertex in its place (MakeSDVertices),
as in a finned rod's common with a union holding it, and as in the
reported union of all 31 bodies, which with a match by identity alone
still missed 24 of them. At a marked vertex Path closes a loop only
at the visit whose point is the one it came to (not a period away from
it), and goes on only along edges leaving that point, as it does at a
vertex on a closed edge with the 2d tolerance. Planar faces, vertices
whose edges meet them at one point, and vertices on a seam are split as
before.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2170.
